.TH MAAK 1 "2026" "maak 0.8.17" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
maak \- run project tasks written in GNU Guile Scheme
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BR maak
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ TASKS ]
.RI [ TASK_ARGS ] ...
.PP
.BR maak
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI \-\- \ TASKS ...
.RI [ \-\- \ TASK_ARGS ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B maak
is a command runner and control plane: instead of a bespoke build DSL it uses
the full GNU Guile Scheme language, so a task is simply a procedure in a file
called a
.IR "Maak file" .
.PP
By default
.B maak
loads
.B ./maak.scm
and runs the task named
.BR default .
A Maak file declares the module
.B (maak)
and imports the library:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
(define\-module (maak)
  #:declarative? #t
  #:use\-module (maak maak))

(define (hello)
  "Say hello from Maak!"
  (display "Hello from Maak!\\n"))
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every procedure the module makes visible is a runnable task.  When the module
declares
.BR #:export ,
only the exported procedures are tasks; other procedures become private
helpers.
.PP
The tasks to run are named on the command line, and the words that follow
them are passed to them as their arguments.  Several tasks may be run in one
invocation -- as one comma separated word, or one word each after a
.B \-\-
-- and a
.B \-\-
may set the arguments apart from the tasks they belong to.  See
.B TASKS AND ARGUMENTS
below.
.SH TASKS AND ARGUMENTS
A run names the tasks it is to run, and passes the words that follow them on
to them as their arguments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak hello                 one task
maak quick\-math 23 42      a task, then its arguments
.EE
.RE
.PP
A
.B \-\-
may set the arguments apart from the task, which is how an argument that
begins with a dash is passed on without being read as an option:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak quick\-math \-\- \-23 42
.EE
.RE
.PP
Several tasks are named as one word, separated by commas:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak clean,build,test
.EE
.RE
.PP
or, when a
.B \-\-
comes before any task word, as one word each:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak \-\- clean build test
.EE
.RE
.PP
So a
.B \-\-
before the first task word ends the options and introduces the task list, and
a
.B \-\-
after a task word ends the task list and starts the arguments of the tasks
named before it.  A run that wants to name its tasks one word each and give
them arguments writes both separators:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak \-\- clean build \-\- \-v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Everything after the separator that begins the arguments is passed on as it
stands:
.B maak
reads no options there, and a word that names one of its own tasks is an
argument like any other.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " FILE
Load
.I FILE
instead of
.BR ./maak.scm .
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-env\-file " " FILE
Read
.B NAME=VALUE
assignments from
.I FILE
before the Maak file is loaded.  The flag may be repeated, or several files
given at once separated by colons, as in
.BR PATH .
Without it,
.B MAAK_ENV_FILE
is read, and failing that a
.B .env
file beside the Maak file.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-env\-file
Read no environment file at all, whatever the environment or the checkout
says.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-directory " " DIR
Change to
.I DIR
before anything else: the Maak file (
.BR \-f ,
the default
.BR ./maak.scm )
and the
.B .env
file beside it are then looked up inside
.IR DIR ,
and the directory entered is reported.  The flag may be repeated, or several
directories given at once separated by colons, and they are entered in the
order given.  A directory that is not there is an error rather than a quiet
fallback, so a task never runs somewhere it was not asked to.
.TP
.B \-\-banner
Print the greeting banner.  This is the default, and is what turns the banner
back on after
.B MAAK_NO_BANNER
was exported.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-banner
Skip the greeting banner, keeping everything else the run would print.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a help screen and exit.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-errors
Carry on after a task fails, and let the run succeed anyway: each failure is
reported where it happened, as a warning.  This is
.B \-\-keep\-going
with a different verdict.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-going
Carry on after a task fails instead of ending the run there.  Each failure is
reported where it happened, naming the task that raised it, and the run then
fails with the list of tasks that did not succeed.  Without this flag the first
failure ends the run, which is what a pipeline of tasks usually wants.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List every task in the loaded Maak file, with its documentation.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-load\-path " " DIR
Add
.I DIR
to Guile's load path so the Maak file can require modules that live outside
its own directory.  The flag may be repeated, or several directories given at
once separated by colons, as in
.BR PATH .
The directory holding the Maak file, and the directories named by
.BR MAAK_LOAD_PATH ,
are added automatically.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the shell commands a task would run instead of running them.  The same
effect is available inside a task by parameterising
.BR dry\-run? .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-print\-directory
Do not report the directories
.B \-C
changes to.  A run says which directory it entered, because a task that ran
somewhere else is worth knowing about; this is the switch that leaves those
lines out, for a run whose output is read by something other than a person.
.BR \-q
silences them as well, among other things.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the banner, the log lines and the header output.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version, the license and the homepage, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Let an error reach Guile's own top level handler so the full backtrace is
printed.  Equivalent to setting
.BR MAAK_DEBUG .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tasks " " LIST
Run the tasks named in
.IR LIST ,
a comma separated list.  The tasks named as words on the command line are
turned into this option, which is what
.B maak
hands to the library; naming the tasks here instead leaves every other word on
the line as an argument for them.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GUILE_LOAD_PATH
Guile's own load path; honoured as usual and extended by
.BR \-L .
.TP
.B MAAK_LOAD_PATH
A colon separated list of extra directories to add to the load path.  Unlike
.BR GUILE_LOAD_PATH
it does not replace anything, which makes it convenient to set per project.
.TP
.B MAAK_ENV_FILE
A colon separated list of environment files to read when
.B \-\-env\-file
is not given.  A name that is already in the environment is left alone.
.TP
.B MAAK_NO_BANNER
When set to a non\-empty value, skip the greeting banner.
.B \-\-banner
overrides it.
.TP
.B MAAK_DEBUG
When set to a non\-empty value, print the Guile backtrace on an error instead
of the short maak message.
.SH TASK AUTHORING
A task is an ordinary procedure.  Its docstring is shown by
.B \-\-list
and before the task runs.
.PP
The following helpers are available from
.BR "(maak maak)" ;
all of them honour dry\-run and quiet modes.
.SS Shell
.TP
.B ($ parts)
Run a shell command built from a list of strings, joined by spaces.  With
.BR #:verbose? " #t"
the command is logged first.
.PP
The command can be decorated with keyword arguments, applied in the order a
shell would write them:
.BR "[sudo] [env A=1] COMMAND [>/dev/null 2>&1] [&]" .
.BR #:env " '((\"CI\" . \"1\"))"
sets names for that command alone, given as an alist, as a list of
.B KEY=VALUE
strings, or as one such string.
.BR #:sudo? " #t"
runs the command as root, and
.BR #:async? " #t"
starts it without waiting for it.
.BR #:dev\-null? " #t"
discards its output.
.BR #:allow\-failure? " #t"
returns
.B #f
instead of raising when the command exits non\-zero, so the task is told the
command failed rather than having the failure hidden from it.  A backgrounded
command is never waited for, so it reports having been started rather than
having succeeded.
.TP
.B (syscall command)
Run
.I command
and raise an error when it exits non\-zero.
.TP
.B (capture command)
Run
.I command
and return its standard output as a string.
.TP
.B (which command)
Return the absolute path of
.IR command ,
or
.BR #f .
.TP
.B (require\-command command)
Like
.BR which ,
but raise a maak error when the command is missing.
.TP
.B (shell\-quote string)
Quote
.I string
so a shell reads it as one literal word.
.SS Reporting a failure
.TP
.B (task\-error message)
Stop the task with
.I message
treated as text with
.B format
directives.  The arguments for those directives are passed with
.BR #:args ,
and what the user should do about it with
.BR #:hint :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
(task-error "no artefact for ~a" #:args (list "linux-x86_64")
            #:hint "Run `maak build' first.")
.EE
.RE
.PP
The message and the hint are printed on stderr without a backtrace, and the
exit status is 1, so a failure reads like one of maak's own.  With
.B \-\-debug
the backtrace is printed as well.
.TP
.B (raise\-maak\-error message #:hint HINT)
The same mechanism for a message that is already formatted.
.TP
.B (maak\-error? exception)
Recognise either of the above once it has been caught.
.SS Files
.BR cp ", " mv ", " mkdir\-p ", " remkdir\-p ", " delete\-file\-recursively ", " cat ", "
.BR touch ", " ln\-s ", " chmod\-file ", " read\-file ", " write\-file
.SS Directory and environment
.BR with\-directory ", " cd ", " env\-get ", " env\-set! ", " env\-unset! ", " with\-env ", "
.BR with\-env\-vars ", " home\-dir ", " tmp\-dir ", " load\-env\-file ", "
.BR env\-file\-entries
.SS Load paths
.BR add\-load\-path! ", " add\-load\-paths! ", " with\-load\-path ", " split\-load\-path ", "
.BR environment\-load\-paths ", " maak\-load\-path
.SS Pretty multiline strings
.BR string\-lines ", " unlines ", " unwords ", " dedent\-string ", " indent\-string ", "
.BR text
.SS Guix
.BR guix\-shell ", " guix\-container ", " guix\-pack ", " manifest\-shell ", " program\-shell ", "
.BR time\-machine ", " time\-machine\-manifest\-shell ", " time\-machine\-program\-shell
.SS Nix
.BR nix\-shell ", " nix\-develop ", " nix\-build ", " nix\-flake\-build ", " nix\-flake\-check ", "
.BR nix\-run
.SS Internationalisation
.BR i18n\-extract ", " i18n\-init ", " i18n\-merge ", " i18n\-compile ", " i18n\-refresh ", "
.BR i18n\-init\-all ", " i18n\-merge\-all ", " i18n\-compile\-all ", " i18n\-translator ", "
.BR i18n\-locale\-dir ", " i18n\-po\-file ", " i18n\-mo\-file ", " i18n\-pot\-file ", "
.BR i18n\-source\-globs ", " i18n\-language
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every requested task finished successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The Maak file was missing, a task failed, or an option was invalid.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ./maak.scm
The default Maak file.
.TP
.B ./.env
Read before the Maak file when no
.B \-\-env\-file
is given and
.B MAAK_ENV_FILE
is unset.  It holds
.B NAME=VALUE
lines; surrounding quotes are removed and comments, blank lines and lines
without an
.B =
are skipped.  A name the environment already has is left alone.
.TP
.B ./channels.scm
The Guix channels file used by the
.B time\-machine
helpers, when present.
.TP
.B ./manifest.scm
The Guix manifest used by the
.B manifest\-shell
helpers, when present.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the default task:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run two tasks in sequence:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak clean,build
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run three tasks, named one word each:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak \-\- clean build test
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pass arguments to a task:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak quick\-math \-\- 23 42
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a task an argument that begins with a dash:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak quick\-math \-\- \-23 42
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the tasks of another file, quietly:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak \-q \-f ./tools/tasks.scm \-\-list
.EE
.RE
.PP
Let the Maak file require modules from
.BR ./lib ,
and dry\-run the release pipeline:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak \-L ./lib \-n \-\- release
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read credentials from a file and skip the greeting banner:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maak \-\-no\-banner \-e .env.production \-\- deploy
.EE
.RE
